WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
After a temporary file is created, the program still has to operate on it safely.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.240
Reads and writes can be partial or fail.

3
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:16.380
Return values should be checked, and the program should know whether it has written all intended data or consumed the complete expected content.

4
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.860
Partial operations should be handled explicitly.

5
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.000
A write that transfers only part of a buffer is not the same as a successful complete write.

6
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:33.020
A read that returns less than expected may be normal end-of-file, a short read, or an error depending on context.

7
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.160
The code should make that distinction before continuing.

8
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.560
Final output should be separated from work files.

9
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:54.220
A temporary file used to build final content should remain in a working state until the program has finished writing, checked errors, performed any required flush or finalization step, and validated that the content matches the expected contract.

10
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.620
Durable replacement patterns should be designed at a high level for the platform and storage system.

11
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:09.560
The important review points are whether the program knows when work is complete, whether finalization errors are handled, and whether the final artifact is exposed only after it is ready.

12
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:24.700
A successful rename may expose the new target before parent-directory synchronization completes; if that synchronization fails, replacement occurred but durability remains unconfirmed, so the API should return a distinct status instead of reporting a pre-commit failure.

13
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.860
Temporary content should be validated before consuming it.

14
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:36.840
If another component writes or reads the file, the consumer should verify size, state, expected source, and format before relying on the content.

15
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:43.260
Temporary-file safety continues past creation and into every read, write, and replacement step.
